# interpreted lane. It was true the day it was written, which is the whole hazard --
# a rationale conditional on an absence stops being true without anything touching
# the line. The same sentence about the same module was already found stale once, in
# `cluster/extra-tests`, where it had kept a file claimed by the wrong module.
#
# The exclusion itself stands, on the half that was never about `cluster` existing:
# the file passes against an emptied module on both lanes, so it cannot measure this
# one. That was found by `--sabotage` rather than by reading.
#
# Re-examined 2026-09-14 rather than assumed. Run under `cluster`'s lane -- where
# both `cluster` and `child_process` are substituted -- it reports `{"kind":"pass"}`
# and **exits 1**, on
#
#     assert.strictEqual(worker.process.channel, null)   actual: undefined
#
# because this profile's `ChildProcess` has no `channel` property at all. That is a
# real divergence and it is recorded rather than fixed, because the demand for it is
# one assertion in this excluded file: across `test/parallel`, `.channel` appears as
# `dc.channel` 16 times (`diagnostics_channel`, unrelated), `process.channel` 4 times
# -- always inside a *child*, where `process` is node's own and it already works --
# and `worker.process.channel` exactly here. Claiming the file for `cluster` instead
# would add a hollow pass, since it is role-guarded on `cluster.isPrimary` and an
# emptied `cluster` takes neither branch.
#
# Removing it lowers this module's count from 81 to 80 on the interpreted lane and
# from 1 to **0** on the compiled lane. That second number is the honest one: the
# compiled lane has no non-hollow pass here at all, and reporting 1 was reporting
# this file.
